    "chunk_text": "The Northern and Eastern Provinces have distinct sectors with export potential, which could contribute meaningfully to the national economy. Let us start one by one. Culinary heritage and agro exports, traditional food items - you know about Jaffna traditional food items - organic products and preserved seafood from these regions are of high demand, especially among the overseas community. With proper training, packaging and marketing support, these items could be developed into powerful export brands. I will take an interesting example. When I visit Jaffna Courts, I go to \"Rasavin Wanam\". Just in front of it, there is a small boutique, which has a sweet item called \"elluppahu\" , a blackball full of nutritional value. You do not get that in any part of the country. So, these have to be promoted. I always go there and have that snack.\n\nThen, handloom and traditional textiles. Our handloom sector is a cultural treasure. It also presents a strong case for premium exports in the global sustainable\n\nThen, Palmyra-based products. Palmyra is abandoned in both provinces and holds immense value from jaggery, vinegar to fibre products and handicrafts. But, this industry remains informal and fragmented. It needs organized support, quality certification and research-led value addition.\n\nAlso, you get seafood and marine products. Our coastal belt, particularly in the North and the East, are rich with exportable seafood like crab, cuttlefish and dried fish. But, we lack a cold chain, export licence and post-harvest processing support necessary to compete internationally."
