    "language": "si-ta-en",
    "md5": "f3096f9b9a53e804917e6c651e3d0212",
    "chunk_size_bytes": 1378,
    "chunk_text": "sovereign bonds, with which this Government has promised to go to the market in 2027. Then, if we take other commercial facilities, that would be, for example, from the HSBC Bank or multilateral agencies like the World Bank, ADB and so on. Let us keep that aside for a moment.\n\nSo, what happened was, the Government guaranteed all kinds of facilities for all kinds of infrastructure projects of those SOEs without looking at whether they were able to pay back those loans. A lot of people say, \"We are out of bankruptcy\" and all that. But, how that is defined is when rating agencies remove a country from their default rating. Yes, Fitch Ratings and Moody\u2019s have done that, but the big one, the S & P - the Standard & Poor's - has still not taken Sri Lanka out of their default rating and the reason for that, Sir, is because the SriLankan Airlines is in default. It has borrowed US Dollars 175 million from the international market. Now, with the interest building up - the debt has not been serviced for the last couple of years - it has gone beyond US Dollars 200 million. So, what the S & P is saying is that they are not able to remove Sri Lanka from default status, meaning bankruptcy in the general language, unless the SriLankan Airlines is able to either restructure this facility or pay the loan. Currently, we are in discussion and I will come to that in a minute."
  },
