    "chunk_size_bytes": 875,
    "chunk_text": "\u0d9c\u0dd4 \u0da2\u0dd3.\u0da2\u0dd3. \u0dc0\u0db4\u0ddc\u0db1\u0dca\u0db1\u0db8\u0dca\u0db6\u0dbd\u0db8\u0dca \u0db8\u0dc4\u0dba\u0dad\u0dcf (\u0bae\u0bbe\u0ba3\u0bcd\u0baa\u0bc1\u0bae\u0bbf\u0b95\u0bc1 \u0b9c\u0bc0.\u0b9c\u0bc0. \u0ba4\u0baa\u0bbe\u0ba9\u0bcd\u0ba9\u0bae\u0bcd\u0baa\u0bb2\u0bae\u0bcd) (The Hon. G.G. Ponnambalam) Hon. Presiding Member, I would like to state at the very outset that, in principle, we are in agreement with the Hon. Minister of Justice and National Integration on bringing in these Amendments, particularly the Amendment to the Code of Criminal Procedure. I think\n\nIn an odd way, it is quite surprising that it has taken this long for some of these Amendments to be brought in. So, in that sense, I would like to congratulate the Hon. Minister. I do think that in processes such as this, there will be shortcomings even with these Amendments; there might be gaps that need to be filled. But, knowing the Minister for what he is, I am pretty certain he will address those in the future."
  },
  {
    "doc_type": "lk_hansard_2020s",
    "doc_id": "2025-05-23-2025-05-23",
    "num": "2025-05-23",
    "date_str": "2025-05-23",
    "description": "Hansard of May 23, 2025",
    "url_metadata": "https://www.parliament.lk/en/business-of-parliament/hansards",
    "lang": "si-ta-en",
    "url_pdf": "https://www.parliament.lk/uploads/documents/hansard/1750228312097834.pdf",
    "chunk_id": "2025-05-23-2025-05-23-0144",
    "chunk_index": 144,
    "language": "si-ta-en",
    "md5": "a5a0668117bf6387e6cbb7b76e07d5b4",
    "chunk_size_bytes": 1603,
    "chunk_text": "In an odd way, it is quite surprising that it has taken this long for some of these Amendments to be brought in. So, in that sense, I would like to congratulate the Hon. Minister. I do think that in processes such as this, there will be shortcomings even with these Amendments; there might be gaps that need to be filled. But, knowing the Minister for what he is, I am pretty certain he will address those in the future.\n\nAnd, without making genocide a crime in this country, to say that it is a crime if anybody asserts that genocide has taken place, I think you cannot get anything more ridiculous than that. If we take this attitude of the Government, on the one hand, they say that they want to be different from the previous governments saying that the previous governments were racist and that that they did wrong and on the other, before the elections, the NPP did not want to talk in terms of war heroes. They said that\n\n1605 1606\n\n\u0db4\u0dcf\u0dbb\u0dca\u0dbd\u0dd2\u0dc1\u0dda\u0db1\u0dca\u0dad\u0dd4\u0dc0\n\nthe term \"war heroes\" must not be used. That was before the elections in order to get votes. But, thereafter, attending a function of the military and then stating things of this nature to the media, in our view, actually runs counter to the Government\u2019s intention or at least, claimed intention of wanting to be genuine about reconciliation. The fact remains, Sir, that the Sri Lankan State, in its prosecution of the war and even before, even in a non-war situation, because of the policies that have been pursued over the last 76 years, has, we believe, systematically dismantled our identity. That is the accusation."
  },
  {
    "doc_type": "lk_hansard_2020s",
