    "md5": "07af246b8a1f1c41be0a42667768153a",
    "chunk_size_bytes": 1918,
    "chunk_text": "In 2022, when this statute was brought in to Parliament, I think under the Gotabaya Rajapaksa Government, there was a little attention paid because there was chaos in the country: there was the COVID-19 pandemic, there was bankruptcy and people were more worried about inflation, food and petroleum. So, they did not really look at this. Though there was a petition filed by the Young Journalists\u2019 Association, the Supreme Court, on a mere technical fault, did not take it up. This tension or confrontation between the State\u2019s right to information - we believe that the State has a right to information - and individual data privacy must be handled with sensitivity and care because we need to preserve the democratic individual civic rights of the people. Otherwise, there would be a dictator regime, which would come and misuse this data. Therefore, this Act has to address these things. That is why when this Act was brought in during the Gotabaya Rajapaksa Government, we got up and said that there had to be amendments. And, we believed that the NPP, which is supposed to be a people\u2019s Government, would be sensitive to the people\u2019s rights. But, I do not see that in the Amendment. That is the issue.\n\nThe shortcomings of the Personal Data Protection Act were described very well by a young researcher, Nida Admani, in the \"DailyFT\" yesterday. She says, I quote:\n\nIf you take the Personal Data Protection Act, to the Data Protection Authority, you would appoint technocrats, business leaders and industry representatives because they all deal with data and have to be special and ingenious with that knowledge. But, what about the appointment of a human rights lawyer, an independent person and a civil society specialist to this Authority? All those appointments would be political appointments. At that time, when we were in the Opposition, we made an\n\n1799 1800\n\n\u0db4\u0dc1\u0dba\u0db7\u0db6\u0dbd\u0dc1\u0db4\u0dad\u0db0\u0dba\u0dad\u0dd4"
  },
  {
    "doc_type": "lk_hansard_2020s",
    "doc_id": "2025-06-03-2025-06-03",
    "num": "2025-06-03",
    "date_str": "2025-06-03",
    "description": "Hansard of June 03, 2025",
    "url_metadata": "https://www.parliament.lk/en/business-of-parliament/hansards",
    "lang": "si-ta-en",
    "url_pdf": "https://www.parliament.lk/uploads/documents/hansard/1750149440002739.pdf",
    "chunk_id": "2025-06-03-2025-06-03-0171",
    "chunk_index": 171,
    "language": "si-ta-en",
    "md5": "d391e10a99ccfc1395d52ada81ac79ce",
    "chunk_size_bytes": 1922,
    "chunk_text": "If you take the Personal Data Protection Act, to the Data Protection Authority, you would appoint technocrats, business leaders and industry representatives because they all deal with data and have to be special and ingenious with that knowledge. But, what about the appointment of a human rights lawyer, an independent person and a civil society specialist to this Authority? All those appointments would be political appointments. At that time, when we were in the Opposition, we made an\n\n1799 1800\n\n\u0db4\u0dc1\u0dba\u0db7\u0db6\u0dbd\u0dc1\u0db4\u0dad\u0db0\u0dba\u0dad\u0dd4\n\nSecondly, Madam, this Act grants broad exemptions to the public sector. The public sector or the public entities, in the name of national security, could use, misuse, collect or abuse data. For example, if it is the national health sector, if there is some very sensitive private information they have collected about people and if there is a breach and that data gets out, it could harm the dignity of a lot of people and affect their private lives, which is not fair. But, public entities can go without answering any of those questions. For example, the Ministry of Defence. Even in the Ministry of Defence, if there is a breach, then it must be subject to certain rules and regulations. If they make a breach, they should disclose that to the public so that the public is aware that something like that had happened. But, the Act does not address that.\n\nSo, if we are committed to protecting privacy, then the Government must be subject to the same standards that the private sector is subject to or if not, to a higher standard.\n\nNext, the Act falls short in empowering certain categories. It does not grant a right to erasure, data portability or the right to challenge decisions made by algorithms. In today\u2019s AI-driven world, these omissions could be more than technical; they could become political. But, the Act that you have brought in does not address those issues."
  },
